    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's hard to be \"gentle\" when there isn't even anything to be gentle to. like i'm just watching the clock tick and waiting for the day to end so i can sleep and not have to deal with the fact that i'm basically a ghost. i look at my hands and they don't feel like mine. it feels like i'm just haunting my own life. is it even possible to build a person from scratch like this? it feels impossible.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is incredibly painful when the self feels detached, like an observer in your own life; this profound sense of numbness is a common symptom of deep grieving. While rebuilding feels monumental right now, please know that the \"you\" from before loss is integrated into the person you are now, even if they are deeply buried. Small, gentle acknowledgments of the present moment—a warm cup of tea, a stretch—are tiny ways of anchoring yourself back into your own physical life.",
      "source": "target",
